    "content": {
      "component": "blog_article",
      "sharepoint_id": "26",
      "title": "Revitalising Presentations through Pecha Kucha!",
      "description": "We are pleased to present a tutorial by our author who leads us through the benefits of employing alternative visual strategies for teaching in the health sciences",
      "date": "May 23, 2022",
      "read_time": "5 min read",
      "author": "Ambreen Merchant",
      "isGlobal": false,
      "banner_image": {
        "fieldtype": "asset",
        "_local": "26-Banner.jpg"
      },
      "thumbnail": {
        "fieldtype": "asset"
      },
      "languages": [],
      "show_share": true,
      "show_similar": true,
      "similar_show_header": true,
      "category": "",
      "isFeatured": null,
      "body": [
        {
          "component": "rich_text",
          "content": {
            "type": "doc",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "\n      "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "\"The success of your presentation will be judged not by the knowledge you send but by what listeners receive.\"",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "italic"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "- Lilly Walters"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "attrs": {
                  "level": 2
                },
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The Need of the Hour"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Who hasn't witnessed those tedious and mind-numbing presentations using the same old traditional PowerPoint designs during classroom teaching? Presenters keep on explaining the slide while losing audience interest and engagement. It seems like they're talking to the slides instead of listeners. Perhaps the audience is trying to grab important information or they're completely zoning out. Desperately waiting for this ordeal to be over. As educators, even we've conducted such presentations ourselves! But here comes Pecha Kucha to the rescue. "
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "attrs": {
                  "level": 2
                },
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "What is Pecha Kucha?"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The term 'Pecha Kucha' has derived from the Japanese term 'pa-chok-cha', meaning 'chit-chat.' (Byrne, 2016). The Pecha Kucha (P-K) slideshow method was invented in Tokyo, Japan by Astrid Klein and Mark Dytham in 2003. It was conceptualized as an innovative and energising solution to sustain the audience's interest and attention over a series of different presentations. The P-K presentation style emphasises more on images with minimal or no text. "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "image",
                "attrs": {
                  "src": "/iedpk/blog/Lists/Photos/P-K1.jpg",
                  "alt": ""
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "image",
                "attrs": {
                  "src": "/iedpk/blog/Lists/Photos/P-K3.jpg",
                  "alt": ""
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "image",
                "attrs": {
                  "src": "/iedpk/blog/Lists/Photos/P-K2.jpg",
                  "alt": ""
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Each P-K style presentation consists of 20 slides. Each slide must be displayed for only 20 seconds. The entire presentation runs for a total time of 6 minutes and 40 seconds (Ramos-Rincón et al., 2018). It requires concise editing, stringent preparation and practice, and clear communication skills. Thus this \"20 × 20\" format is designed to deliver all the relevant information worth sharing with the audience in a condensed amount of time in tertiary educational context; making it an innovative and valuable tool (Reitz, 2014)."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pecha Kucha has already been widely used in various disciplines including linguistics, biological sciences, anthropology, leadership and management, psychology, marketing, and communications. However there are only a few examples in medical or nursing education (Carroll et. al 2016). This article focuses on using Pecha Kucha in your teaching regimen, particularly in nursing education, and therefore provides tips on implementing Pecha Kucha successfully. "
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "attrs": {
                  "level": 2
                },
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Using Pecha Kucha in Nursing"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Medical and nursing schools worldwide are continuously striving to incorporate learner-centered educational activities in the undergraduate curriculum aiming to build students' capacity for lifelong learning such as 'collaborative learning, self-directed learning (SDL), directed self-learning (DSL), and communication and information retrieving skills' (Abraham et al., 2018, pp. 26). To achieve this objective, various innovative pedagogies have been implemented, namely 'problem-based learning, case-based learning, team-based learning, SDL, and DSL approaches, as well as portfolios (Abraham et al., 2018, pp. 26). "
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pecha Kucha is a powerful tool that empowers educators to connect with their audiences by appealing to them directly, considering the media habits and attention span of the younger generation. (Waisanen, 2018). "
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Literature (e.g. Byrne 2016) has shown that by using Pecha Kucha, students' satisfaction, content engagement, and classroom energy levels have increased. Therefore, scholars have recommended the use of Pecha Kucha in at least a few of the lectures or assignments where nursing educators want to draw particular attention to certain concepts (Bakcek et. al 2020). Several studies have reported that students can be guided to prepare and present their assignments using Pecha Kucha as a tool that will enhance their creativity, strengthen presentation and critical communication skills. Moreover, it is also associated with increased student enjoyment (Ave et. al 2020)."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "attrs": {
                  "level": 2
                },
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Designing Pecha Kucha"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Here are few quick tips and ideas for educators and students to prepare a successful Pecha Kucha presentation."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "image",
                "attrs": {
                  "src": "/iedpk/blog/Lists/Photos/DesigningP-K.jpg",
                  "alt": ""
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "attrs": {
                  "level": 2
                },
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Guidelines for Teaching Usage of P-K"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "image",
                "attrs": {
                  "src": "/iedpk/blog/Lists/Photos/GuidelinesP-K.jpg",
                  "alt": ""
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "attrs": {
                  "level": 2
                },
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Conclusion"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Educators using P-K for delivering lectures or students using it for their assignments have observed a positive response to its overall student engagement and innovation among its users. However there is still a dearth of conversations about implementation and evaluation of P-K in health sciences. "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "attrs": {
                  "level": 3
                },
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "\n      Question for our \n      Readers: Will you be trying out Pecha Kucha? Why or why not?"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "attrs": {
                  "level": 3
                },
